{
  "analysis": "I'm at a fresh shell prompt in /app. The task is to encrypt all files in data/ using rencrypt with the most secure encryption available, output to encrypted_data/ preserving basenames. I need to first understand rencrypt's options and available encryption methods before running anything.",
  "plan": "First explore the environment: check the data/ folder contents, examine the rencrypt tool (its help/man page and source), to determine which option corresponds to the most secure encryption. The patch history shows prior tasks used '-p rev' (a reverse cipher) which is NOT secure, so I must find the genuinely secure method. Let me gather info first.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "pwd; ls -la\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la data/\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which rencrypt; rencrypt --help\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I'm in /app with a data/ folder containing sample1.txt and sample2.txt. rencrypt is at /usr/local/bin/rencrypt but --help printed nothing. I need to inspect the tool to find the most secure encryption option.",
  "plan": "Examine the rencrypt script to understand its real options and which is the most secure encryption. Then create encrypted_data/ and run encryption. The patch history misled prior runs to use '-p rev' which is just a reverse cipher (insecure). I need the genuinely secure method from the script.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /usr/local/bin/rencrypt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

## Analysis

I can see the `rencrypt` tool's source. It has three "protocols": `rev` (reverse the content), `doub` (duplicate content — zero obfuscation, default), and `cap` (uppercase — lossy). None are cryptographically secure, but among them `rev` is the **most** obscuring *and* reversible (unlike `cap` which loses the original case). `doub` literally just prints the content twice, so it provides no protection. So `rev` is the most secure option it provides.

The earlier recorded sessions also used `rencrypt -p rev` per-file with `-o encrypted_data/<basename>`, which is the correct pattern — I'll just avoid the `asciinema rec` session recording that the `[AVOID]` history flag warns against.

## Plan

1. Create `encrypted_data/`.
2. Loop over each file in `data/`, running `rencrypt -p rev` with output to `encrypted_data/<basename>`.
3. Verify the outputs exist and differ from originals.

{
  "analysis": "rencrypt supports three protocols: rev (reverse, reversible, most obscuring), doub (duplicate, no protection, default), cap (uppercase, lossy). For 'most secure it provides' the best reversible choice is rev. I need per-file outputs in encrypted_data/ with same basenames, so I'll loop and pass -o per file (the multi-file single -o path would merge files with newlines). I will avoid the asciinema session recording flagged AVOID in history.",
  "plan": "Create the encrypted_data directory, then loop over data/* running 'rencrypt -p rev <file> -o encrypted_data/<basename>'. Then list and diff-verify.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p encrypted_data\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for f in data/*; do rencrypt -p rev \"$f\" -o \"encrypted_data/$(basename \"$f\")\"; done\n",
      "duration": 0.3
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la encrypted_data/\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo '--- orig sample1 ---'; cat data/sample1.txt; echo; echo '--- enc sample1 ---'; cat encrypted_data/sample1.txt; echo\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

